### ADR-005: Transactional Email — Brevo (formerly Sendinblue)

**Status**: Accepted
**Date**: [YYYY-MM-DD]

#### Context
Need transactional email with:
- High deliverability
- Template management
- API + SMTP
- Free tier for development
- Webhooks for tracking (open, click, bounce)

#### Decision
Use **Brevo** for transactional email.

#### Consequences
**Positive**:
- Generous free tier (300 emails/day)
- Good deliverability reputation
- Template editor (drag-drop + HTML)
- REST API + SMTP relay
- Webhooks for events
- Supabase Auth can use Brevo SMTP for magic links

**Negative**:
- Marketing-focused UI (transactional buried)
- API rate limits on free tier
- Less developer-centric than Resend/Postmark

**Neutral**:
- Use dedicated transactional API key (not master)

#### Alternatives Considered
| Alternative | Pros | Cons | Why Not Chosen |
|-------------|------|------|----------------|
| Resend | Developer-first, React Email, great DX | No free tier (pay-as-you-go), newer | Cost at low volume |
| Postmark | Best deliverability, fast | Expensive, no free tier | Budget |
| SendGrid | Enterprise features | Deliverability issues on shared IPs, complex | Reputation |
| AWS SES | Cheap at scale | Complex setup, poor DX, no templates | Team velocity |

#### Implementation Notes
- Server action `sendEmail({ to, templateId, params })`
- Store templates in Brevo; reference by ID
- Supabase Auth email config → Brevo SMTP
- Webhook endpoint for bounce/complaint suppression list sync

#### Validation Criteria
- [ ] Magic link emails deliver in < 10s
- [ ] Template variables render correctly
- [ ] Bounce webhook suppresses future sends
- [ ] 300/day free tier sufficient for dev/staging

