## 4. Decision-Making Framework

### 4.1 Reversibility Classification
| Type | Examples | Process |
|------|----------|---------|
| **Type 1 (Irreversible)** | Database choice, auth provider, hosting platform | Full ADR, team consensus, documented alternatives |
| **Type 2 (Reversible with cost)** | Component library, state management, email provider | Lightweight ADR, 1-2 day spike, reversible |
| **Type 3 (Easily reversible)** | Linting rules, folder structure, naming conventions | PR discussion, no ADR needed |

### 4.2 When to Create an ADR
- [ ] New external dependency (service, library)
- [ ] Data model change affecting multiple teams
- [ ] API contract change (breaking)
- [ ] Infrastructure provisioning
- [ ] Security/authentication change
- [ ] Performance-critical path decision

### 4.3 ADR Lifecycle
1. **Draft** — Author creates PR with ADR template
2. **Review** — Team async review (48h), sync if needed
3. **Decide** — Owner merges (accepts) or closes (rejects)
4. **Implement** — Link PRs/issues to ADR
5. **Validate** — Check validation criteria post-deploy
6. **Deprecate** — New ADR supersedes; old marked `Superseded`

